# The README demo, as a script rather than a recording.
#
#   vhs docs/demo.tape        →  docs/demo.gif
#
# A hand-recorded GIF is stale the first time the CLI output changes and nobody
# notices until a reader points at a column that no longer exists. This is
# regenerated by one command, so keeping it honest costs nothing.
#
# vhs: https://github.com/charmbracelet/vhs  (go install github.com/charmbracelet/vhs@latest)
#
# Every Sleep carries an explicit unit. A bare number is read as seconds, so
# `Sleep 1200` written meaning "1.2 seconds" is twenty minutes of vhs sitting
# at a blank prompt with no indication anything is wrong.
#
# The sequence is chosen to make one point: WebFetch gets nothing from this
# page, and sieve gets the site. Everything else is cut.
#
# No block count is named on purpose. igloo.inc's scene carries decorative and
# duplicated objects, so the number varies between twenty-three and twenty-nine
# runs of text depending on how much of the page the sweep reached. What does
# not vary is the ground truth: 40 of 40 facts, every run. A demo should assert
# the number that holds.

Output docs/demo.gif

Set Shell bash
Set FontSize 17
Set Width 1180
Set Height 620
Set Padding 22
Set Theme "Catppuccin Mocha"
Set TypingSpeed 45ms
Set PlaybackSpeed 1.0

# --- What a plain fetch sees -------------------------------------------------
Type "# igloo.inc serves an empty <body>. Everything is drawn in WebGL."
Enter
Sleep 1200ms

Type "curl -s https://igloo.inc | tr -d '[:space:]' | wc -c"
Enter
Sleep 2500ms

Type "# Fourteen characters. No words at all. This is what WebFetch gets."
Enter
Sleep 1800ms

Ctrl+L

# --- What sieve sees ---------------------------------------------------------
Type "sieve distill https://igloo.inc"
Enter

# Wait for the shell prompt to come back, rather than for any particular text.
#
# A fixed sleep is wrong in both directions here: igloo.inc takes anywhere from
# fifteen to forty seconds depending on how long its scene takes to build and
# how patiently the sweep waits for text that is never coming, so a short sleep
# clips the output and a long one records dead air.
#
# Matching on output is worse still. Waiting for the last line the command
# prints fails whenever that line has scrolled off a 620-pixel screen, which is
# most of the time and looks exactly like the command having failed. The prompt
# returning is the one signal that means "finished" and is always on screen.
Wait@90s

Type "# Read out of the 3D scene. 40 of 40 ground-truth facts, every run."
Enter
Sleep 2000ms

Ctrl+L

Type "head -24 artifacts/igloo.inc/index.md"
Enter
Sleep 5000ms

Sleep 2500ms
